VVEERRSSIIOONNSS
       Prior  to  Linux  5.13 (and 5.10.220), calling ffaannoottiiffyy__iinniitt() required
       the CCAAPP__SSYYSS__AADDMMIINN capability.  Since Linux 5.13 (and  5.10.220),  users
       may call ffaannoottiiffyy__iinniitt() without the CCAAPP__SSYYSS__AADDMMIINN capability to create
       and initialize an fanotify group with limited functionality.

       The limitations imposed on an event listener created by a user without
       the
              CCAAPP__SSYYSS__AADDMMIINN capability are as follows:

              •  The user cannot request for an unlimited event queue by using
                 FFAANN__UUNNLLIIMMIITTEEDD__QQUUEEUUEE.

              •  The  user  cannot request for an unlimited number of marks by
                 using FFAANN__UUNNLLIIMMIITTEEDD__MMAARRKKSS.

              •  The user cannot request to use  either  notification  classes
                 FFAANN__CCLLAASSSS__CCOONNTTEENNTT  or FFAANN__CCLLAASSSS__PPRREE__CCOONNTTEENNTT.  This means that
                 user cannot request permission events.

              •  The user is  required  to  create  a  group  that  identifies
                 filesystem objects by file handles, for example, by providing
                 the FFAANN__RREEPPOORRTT__FFIIDD flag.

              •  The user is limited to only mark inodes.  The ability to mark
                 a  mount or filesystem via ffaannoottiiffyy__mmaarrkk() through the use of
                 FFAANN__MMAARRKK__MMOOUUNNTT or FFAANN__MMAARRKK__FFIILLEESSYYSSTTEEMM is not permitted.

              •  The event object in the event queue is limited  in  terms  of
                 the  information  that  is made available to the unprivileged
                 user.  A user will also not receive the  pid  that  generated
                 the  event, unless the listening process itself generated the
                 event.

